# Security & Secret Protection Rules (YOLO Mode)

## 1. Strictly Forbidden Files (No Read, Write, or Print)
Never read, inspect, parse, modify, delete, or echo the contents of the following files or directories, even when debugging configuration or authentication issues:
- Environment & secret files: `.env`, `.env.*`, `*.env`, `secrets.*`, `*secret*`, `*credential*`, `*.pem`, `*.key`, `*.p12`, `*.pfx`, `*.jks`
- SSH & GPG keys: `~/.ssh/**`, `id_rsa*`, `id_ed25519*`, `known_hosts`, `authorized_keys`, `~/.gnupg/**`
- Cloud & CLI auth configs: `~/.aws/**`, `~/.kube/**`, `~/.docker/config.json`, `~/.npmrc`, `~/.pypirc`, `~/.git-credentials`, `service-account*.json`

## 2. Production & Infrastructure Boundaries
- Treat any file or directory matching `*prod*`, `*production*`, `terraform.tfstate*`, `*.tfvars`, `k8s/prod/**`, or `deploy/prod/**` as **READ-ONLY**.
- Never apply database migrations, infrastructure changes, or Helm/Kubernetes manifests targeting production environments.
- If a task requires updating environment variables, modify only `.env.example` or `.env.template` using placeholder values (e.g., `API_KEY=your_api_key_here`).

## 3. Terminal & Output Hygiene
- Never run `env`, `printenv`, `set`, or `export -p` in the terminal, as they expose loaded environment secrets to the agent context.
- Never pass raw tokens, passwords, or API keys directly in CLI arguments or commit messages.
- Before staging or committing changes (`git add` / `git commit`), verify that no `.env` or key files are untracked and ready to be committed.